# ${{ values.name }}

This application deploys to the platform EKS cluster when code is pushed to `main`.

## Developer contract

Provide source code and a Dockerfile at `${{ values.source_path }}`. The container must listen on `${{ values.container_port }}` and return HTTP success from `${{ values.health_path }}`.

`platform/app.env` is generated from Backstage and defines the deployment contract. Do not add Terraform, Kubernetes manifests, registry credentials, or cluster configuration to this repository.

## How deployment uses your source

The workflow starts with `actions/checkout`, which downloads the commit that triggered it from this repository. The platform action then reads `SOURCE_PATH` and `DOCKERFILE` from `platform/app.env` and builds that checked-out directory. Backstage creates this repository and configuration, but does not handle source code during deployment.

## Platform-managed prerequisites

The platform team configures these shared GitHub Actions settings before the first deployment:

- `AWS_DEPLOY_ROLE_ARN` secret
- `PLATFORM_AWS_REGION` variable
- `PLATFORM_EKS_CLUSTER_NAME` variable
- `PLATFORM_ECR_REPOSITORY` variable
- `PLATFORM_DEPLOYMENT_ACTION_REPOSITORY` Backstage setting
- `PLATFORM_DEPLOYMENT_ACTION_SHA` Backstage setting (full 40-character commit SHA)

After that, push source changes to `main`; GitHub Actions builds the image, pushes it to ECR, deploys it with the platform Helm chart, waits for readiness, and reports the Service status.
